{
  format: 1
  name: string-search-absent-argument-rejects-not-false
  suite: scenario
  spec: ["#expressions", "§6.5", "#annex-a", "§A.6"]
  tags: ["boundary"]
  note: '''
    Attack: hand a search predicate an ABSENT `optional<text>` and see whether the
    engine invents an answer. A `false` here would be silently wrong in both
    directions — a guard `string.starts_with(.code, .parent)` on a row with no
    parent would read as "the invariant is violated", while a rule spelled
    `!string.starts_with(...)` would read as "satisfied" — so the engine must not
    guess.

    §6.5: every argument must be a PRESENT `text`; an absent (`none`) argument is
    a typed evaluation error that rejects the containing evaluation with a
    diagnostic, and the predicates never answer `false` for an argument they
    could not compare, nor treat `none` as the empty string. The `none` is read
    from state (`parent` is absent in $data), so the fault is detected at
    evaluation and the call is rejected — the same shape Annex A.6 gives a zero
    divisor read from state.

    The guarded spelling remains admissible and is covered by the sibling common
    case; this case is only about the UNGUARDED call.
  '''

  package: {
    $liasse: 1
    $app: "t.strnone@1.0.0"
    $model: {
      code: "text"
      parent: "text?"
      $mut: {
        probe: "return string.starts_with(.code, .parent)"
      }
      $public: {
        chart: {
          $mut: { probe: ".probe" }
        }
      }
    }
    $data: {
      code: "512100"
    }
  }

  steps: [
    { call: "public.chart.probe", args: {},
      expect: { outcome: rejected, violates: ["#expressions", "§6.5"] } }
  ]
}
